WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.567
The Hessian.

2
00:00:01.867 --> 00:00:06.700
It captures the curvature of the loss surface, and it explains why we reach for Adam.

3
00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:10.667
The Hessian is the matrix of second derivatives.

4
00:00:10.967 --> 00:00:14.933
Where the gradient gives a direction, the Hessian gives the shape of the terrain.

5
00:00:15.233 --> 00:00:20.333
With n parameters it is n by n, so you never build it for a billion parameter model.

6
00:00:21.533 --> 00:00:23.767
Its eigenvalues reveal the terrain.

7
00:00:24.067 --> 00:00:26.333
All positive means a bowl, a minimum.

8
00:00:26.633 --> 00:00:29.033
All negative means a dome, a maximum.

9
00:00:29.333 --> 00:00:33.467
Mixed signs mean a saddle point, down in one direction and up in another.

10
00:00:34.667 --> 00:00:37.867
Take a simple valley, x squared plus three y squared.

11
00:00:38.167 --> 00:00:39.733
Its Hessian is two by two.

12
00:00:40.033 --> 00:00:41.800
Along x the curvature is two.

13
00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:44.688
Along y it is six, three times steeper.

14
00:00:44.688 --> 00:00:49.467
The ratio of the biggest to smallest eigenvalue is the condition number.

15
00:00:50.667 --> 00:00:52.133
Compare the two directions.

16
00:00:52.433 --> 00:00:53.855
One steep, one shallow.

17
00:00:53.855 --> 00:01:00.100
A single global learning rate is always wrong for one of them, which gives slow, zig zagging descent.

18
00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:03.500
In NumPy, we write the Hessian down.

19
00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:07.800
Then ask for its eigenvalues, which give the curvature per direction.

20
00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:09.508
Six over two is three.

21
00:01:09.508 --> 00:01:12.133
One global learning rate cannot fit both.

22
00:01:13.333 --> 00:01:14.500
Here is the payoff.

23
00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:19.533
Training the exoplanet classifier with plain SGD, the loss zig zags.

24
00:01:19.833 --> 00:01:26.767
Raw flux and ratio features have wildly different scales, so the valley is a thin trench with a high condition number.

25
00:01:27.067 --> 00:01:32.367
Adam scales steps per parameter, a cheap stand in for curvature, and walks straight down.

26
00:01:33.567 --> 00:01:34.293
Let's recap.

27
00:01:34.293 --> 00:01:36.533
The Hessian holds second derivatives.

28
00:01:36.833 --> 00:01:40.600
Its eigenvalue signs reveal a bowl, a dome, or a saddle.

29
00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:43.633
A high condition number means zig zag descent.

30
00:01:43.933 --> 00:01:46.667
And Adam adapts step sizes per direction.

31
00:01:46.667 --> 00:01:48.200
Next up: Bayes theorem.
